# Kit, described in Kit's own notation.
#
# kit:project kit
#   kit#237: Kit is one project. This file is its command line; Kit shows the three files
#   as ONE page whose features are its pages and areas, never the files (kit#246),
#   while check and report still read each file on its own.
#
# kit:repo jemmy8oy-northstar/kit@dev
#   BEH-HEALTH-1: where this app's tests live. Hosted Kit reads them from there to count
#   coverage; no line, no number — and the page says why.
# kit:promote self
# kit:switch FEAT-PROJECTS testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-PROJECT testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-BOARD testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-STAGES testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-EPICS testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-INBOX testers on
# kit:switch FEAT-SETTINGS testers on
#
# kit:no-ui
#   Read by saturation.js, which excludes this corpus from the binding-saturation
#   study. That study asks whether the glue binding a spec to a UI saturates as
#   the UI grows; this corpus describes a command-line tool, so its nouns
#   (command:KitCheck, status:One) will never be bound to a locator and it has no
#   answer to give. The directive lives here rather than in a filename list
#   inside saturation.js so that it travels with the file that needs it.
#
# kit:layer technical
#   Every behaviour below describes the command line, never a screen, so the
#   whole file is the technical layer (kit#89, kit#193). That is the reason for
#   the 0% below, stated in the notation rather than only in this prose.
#
# ── Principles (kit#284 slice 5) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Kit is the platform every spec is written in, so its notation principles hold for
# every project: they are `global`. James, kit#285 (2026-10-15): "Over time we need to
# refine it with a few things in mind. Flexibility… function… readability."
#
# kit:principle global Flexibility: the spec is easy to change — a new idea is one optional line, and nothing written before it moves
# kit:principle global Function: the spec turns into tests — every line Kit reads is parsed, refused when wrong, and gated
# kit:principle global Readability: a human can read the spec without Kit — a line says what it means in words
# kit:wake 2026-11-23T22:38:25Z

# James asked (claude-code-bot#89): "think about how kit looks as a kit managed
# project". This corpus is the attempt, and the attempt is the measurement.
#
# ⚠️ READ THE NUMBER THIS PRODUCES CAREFULLY. `kit report kit` generates nothing
# — with the layer above it says 0/0, "every behaviour is technical"; before the
# tag it said 0% — and that is NOT the same finding as james-habits-app's 0%. There,
# the app was missing nouns and Kit was working as designed. Here, every noun
# could be bound tomorrow and the number would not move, because the generator
# emits Playwright and Kit has no browser to point it at. The eight verbs it
# knows — opens, activates, sees, shows, attaches, lands, fills, state — are all
# browser verbs. A command-line tool has none of them.
#
# So this file is not a corpus that is waiting for bindings. It is evidence for
# a claim: **Kit can describe itself and can generate nothing for itself**, and
# the missing piece is not notation, it is a UI. That is the same UI James asked
# for in the first half of #89, which makes his two asks one ask.
#
# ── ✅ THAT PREDICTION HAS NOW BEEN TESTED, AND IT HELD ─────────────────────
# The UI shipped (#24 → #26 → #27 → #28) and `behaviours/kit-ui.beh` describes
# it. Kit derives **20 Playwright steps** for that corpus where this one still
# derives 0, and **5 of the 6 generated tests PASS against the running app**.
# So the number here is genuinely about the verbs and not about this file's
# authoring — the same author, same notation and same generator produce runnable
# tests the moment the subject has a browser.
#
# ⚠️ **THIS FILE'S 0 IS STILL THE FINDING AND MUST NOT BE "FIXED".** Do not add
# browser behaviours here to make the number move. The 0 is what makes the
# contrast measurable, and kit-ui.beh exists so that nobody has to.
# Full account: docs/pilots/kit-self-hosting.md.
#
# Every behaviour below is real, and every one is tested: `kit.tests.json` maps
# each to the C# test that asserts it (backend/Balenthiran.Kit.Tests), and CI's
# `kit check kit` fails the build if one loses its test. (`kit.test.js`, which
# this line used to name, went with the Node engine in kit#202.)

# ═══ Features: the command line's areas (kit#246) ════════════════════════════
# Kit is one project (kit#237), and James asked that nothing on its page read as a
# file. So the command line's areas are features beside the UI's pages. A tag says
# where a behaviour is SHOWN; it is not a dependency (that is `serves`).

feature FEAT-GATE "Gate"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-READER "Reader"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-PARSER "Parser"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-GENERATOR "Generator"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-RESOLVER "Resolver"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-SERVER "Local server"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-WRITER "Writer"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-SCHEDULE "Schedule"
  stage ui
feature FEAT-COST "Cost"
  stage logic

behaviour BEH-GATE-1 "A behaviour with no test naming it fails the build"
  in FEAT-GATE
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:SnipIt
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:One
  contract this is stage 0's stated deliverable in docs/timeline.md

behaviour BEH-GATE-2 "Could-not-look is never reported as green"
  in FEAT-GATE
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given repo:WithNoTests
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:Two
  contract exit 2 is distinct from 0 AND from 1 — a gate that reads nothing and
  contract exits 0 is indistinguishable in CI from a passing one

behaviour BEH-GATE-3 "A mapping naming a test that no longer exists is a hard failure"
  in FEAT-GATE
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given mapping:Stale
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:One
  contract this checkability is the whole argument for option C over markers

behaviour BEH-READ-1 "The reader refuses when its two counts disagree"
  in FEAT-READER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given file:CountsDisagree
  when runs command:KitCheck
  then exits status:Two
  contract the two counts must be different MECHANISMS — position versus lexical
  contract structure — or agreement between them proves nothing

behaviour BEH-GEN-1 "An unbound noun is refused, and the missing noun is named"
  in FEAT-GENERATOR
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:UnboundNoun
  when runs command:KitGenerate
  then reports marker:Ungenerated
  contract no locator is ever guessed from the noun's own name

behaviour BEH-GEN-2 "An unsupplied route param is refused rather than truncated"
  in FEAT-GENERATOR
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:RouteParam
  when runs command:KitGenerate
  then reports marker:Ungenerated
  contract emitting ./editor/ would navigate to the wrong page and look correct

behaviour BEH-PARSE-1 "An unrecognised keyword is an error, not silently dropped"
  in FEAT-PARSER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:BadKeyword
  when runs command:KitParse
  then raises error:UnrecognisedKeyword
  contract a spec language that ignores what it does not understand is how a
  contract spec becomes descriptive

behaviour BEH-PARSE-2 "A parent tag that points nowhere, or loops, is an error naming its line"
  in FEAT-PARSER
  source defined kit#237
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:BadFeatureTags
  when runs command:KitParse
  then raises error:BadFeatureTag
  contract three refusals: an `in` naming no feature in the project, a feature that is its own ancestor,
  contract and a feature id declared twice; silence on any of them leaves a behaviour drawn nowhere
  contract a tag is not a dependency: `in` names only FEAT- ids and `serves` only BEH- ids, so moving a
  contract card on a page can never change what Kit refuses to delete

behaviour BEH-PARSE-3 "A priority is 1 to 5, set once; anything else is an error naming its line"
  in FEAT-PARSER
  source defined kit#284
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:BadPriority
  when runs command:KitParse
  then raises error:BadPriority
  contract 1 is first and 5 is last, the decision queue's scale; a second `priority` line is refused, not silently last-wins
  contract no line is no priority, and nothing is written for it, so no corpus that predates it changes shape

behaviour BEH-PARSE-4 "A review request must say what to look at"
  in FEAT-PARSER
  source defined kit#284
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:BareReviewRequest
  when runs command:KitParse
  then raises error:ReviewRequestWithoutWhat
  contract `review requested <what>` is the agent asking for his eyes on built work; like a denial, a bare one is refused
  contract it is explicit, so a later `source inferred` line does not reset it to unreviewed

behaviour BEH-IDEA-API-1 "A `# kit:idea` file is flagged as ideas in the project list and view"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  source defined kit#284
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:IdeasFile
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports project:Idea
  contract true or absent, never false, so no response that predates it changes shape
  contract only the directive line counts; a comment that mentions kit:idea does not

behaviour BEH-PRIN-API-1 "A `# kit:principle [global] <Name>: <what it means>` line is a principle in the project view"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  source defined kit#284
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:PrincipleLines
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports project:Principles
  contract in file order, each with its `<corpus>.beh:<line>`, because the file is the only place one is changed
  contract absent when a file declares none, so no response that predates it changes shape
  contract a line that does not say a name and what it means is refused, never skipped: a principle that silently vanished is one nobody knows was lost
  contract only the directive line counts; a comment that mentions kit:principle does not

behaviour BEH-RESOLVE-1 "A hole in one behaviour is filled by a different behaviour"
  in FEAT-RESOLVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:CrossBehaviourHole
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports slot:Filled
  contract this is the part no BDD tool has, and it needs ONE symbol table

behaviour BEH-RESOLVE-2 "Two behaviours disagreeing is a conflict, with both sides named"
  in FEAT-RESOLVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:Disagreement
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports conflict:BothSides

behaviour BEH-ADJ-1 "An inference is included by default and marked unreviewed"
  in FEAT-RESOLVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given corpus:Inference
  when runs command:KitResolve
  then reports review:Unreviewed
  contract James's call on claude-code-bot#68 — default-include, marked

# ── the read API (docs/design/ui.md) ────────────────────────────────────────
# The first behaviours here that describe something with a NETWORK surface, and
# the first two are security properties. A security property asserted only in a
# comment above the code is a wish; described here it is named by a test, gated
# by check.js, and mutated by mutate.js.

behaviour BEH-UI-1 "A verb the server has no meaning for reaches no handler"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given request:Unsupported
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:MethodNotAllowed
  contract decision 2 landed on 2026-09-08, so POST now has a handler and this
  contract behaviour narrowed rather than disappeared — PUT, PATCH and DELETE have
  contract one meaning each that nobody has decided, and a route accepting a verb it
  contract cannot serve is a 405 waiting to be discovered as a 500

behaviour BEH-UI-2 "The server binds the loopback interface, not every interface"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given option:Default
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports host:Loopback
  contract 0.0.0.0 would publish every corpus in the working tree, and the absolute
  contract path of every repo beside it, to anything that can reach the host

behaviour BEH-UI-3 "A traversal in an app name is refused rather than resolved to a path"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given request:Traversal
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:NotFound
  contract the name is looked UP in the set of corpora that exist; it is never
  contract joined to a path, so there is nothing to traverse with

behaviour BEH-UI-4 "Coverage that could not be read is null in the list, never zero"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given repo:Absent
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports coverage:Unavailable
  contract project.js's rule, carried through the transport — a UI that cannot tell
  contract "no mapping exists" from "nothing is covered" renders the second

behaviour BEH-UI-7 "The pipeline runs page lists the most recent run first"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 3
  actor engineer
  review unreviewed
  given repo:Present
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports order:NewestFirst
  contract my assumption: the runs page is ordered by when each run started, newest
  contract at the top; the older runs follow, and paging does not reverse that order
  contract my assumption: this card is placed under the server area because the spec
  contract for the runs page is not in this file; check with the owner

behaviour BEH-UI-6 "A ticket keeps its state column and sits by priority, blocked tickets first"
  in FEAT-SERVER
  priority 3
  actor engineer
  given board:Tickets
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports column:State
  contract my assumption: tickets keep their state columns; each column orders its tickets by priority
  contract my assumption: within one priority, a blocked ticket appears above the rest
  contract my assumption: "ticket" means the cards on the board, and their priority is the 1 to 5 of BEH-PARSE-3
  contract built on the pipeline's tickets (BEH-ORCH-16), not the cards: a card's columns are BEH-BOARD-18's, and a blocked tag beside unblocked tickets of one state exists only on a ticket. The third line above says cards; deny it to say which is true
  contract my assumption: the columns are the ticket states that have open tickets, in the order new, ready, planned, built, stopped, and an unlisted state goes last; merged is off the board
  contract built (ui/src/tickets.ts ticketColumns, ui/src/components/Tickets.tsx): each state is a column of the Tickets section, ordered by priority 1 first and within one priority blocked first
  review requested conflict with BEH-ORCH-16 (built, slice 4), which lists every blocked ticket first across the whole Tickets section; this card builds priority first, blocked within one priority, per state column. Approve to keep this reading; deny to say which is true and I rebuild to it

# ── the corpus writer (writer.js) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Decision 2 in docs/design/ui.md lapsed 2026-09-08 and its default became the
# decision: write the file, never touch git. These are the rules that decision
# rests on, and they are the first behaviours here describing something that
# CHANGES a file rather than reading one.

behaviour BEH-WRITE-1 "An edit splices one block, so every comment in the corpus survives"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given corpus:WithComments
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then reports comment:Preserved
  contract a round-trip through parse() would delete every comment in the file, and
  contract snip-it.beh's nine opening lines are the caveat everything else rests on

behaviour BEH-WRITE-2 "An edit that would not parse never reaches the disk"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given step:Malformed
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then raises error:WouldNotParse
  contract the corpus is the source of truth for everything else Kit says, so a
  contract writer that can corrupt it is worse than one that cannot write at all

behaviour BEH-WRITE-3 "An edit that would change a neighbouring behaviour is refused"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given splice:OffByOne
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then raises error:CollateralChange
  contract a step landing in the next behaviour parses perfectly and is silently
  contract wrong — the worst failure a writer can have

behaviour BEH-WRITE-4 "The writer never touches git, and the file proves it rather than promising it"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given decision:LapsedDefault
  when runs command:KitWrite
  then reports commit:None
  contract a bot that commits to a spec repo cannot be un-added after the first
  contract surprise, so the absence of child_process is asserted from source

behaviour BEH-WRITE-5 "A write route is unreachable unless the server is bound to loopback"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given host:Routable
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports status:Forbidden
  contract decision 1 said local tool; combining reachable-from-the-network with
  contract edits-files-in-the-working-tree is an unauthenticated remote write, and
  contract it would arrive as a --host flag rather than as a decision of his

behaviour BEH-WRITE-6 "A hostile page gets no CORS header, so it cannot write through the browser"
  in FEAT-WRITER
  actor engineer
  source defined
  priority 5
  given origin:Hostile
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports header:Absent
  contract access-control-allow-origin: * was defensible while the server could
  contract not write; with a write route it means any page the developer has open
  contract can edit their working tree

behaviour BEH-SCHED-1 "Each pipeline stage runs on its own schedule, independent of the others"
  in FEAT-SCHEDULE
  actor engineer
  source defined
  review unreviewed
  priority 3
  given stage:Triage
  when runs schedule:Own
  then reports wake:Independent
  contract my assumption: each pipeline stage has its own wake schedule and runs when that schedule fires, not when another stage runs
  contract my assumption: an idle or slow stage does not delay or skip the other stages
  contract built: the wake of each stage is its own start. A note starts triage, and each job of BEH-ORCH-11 starts its stage on its own tick, so a build waits as a planned ticket until its tick (switch KIT_PIPELINE_JOB_BUILD on; off by default, so production still chains triage to spec and build)
  review requested conflict between this card's second assumption and BEH-ORCH-3 and BEH-ORCH-14 in kit-ui.beh: those keep one live run per project, so a triage waits while a build on its project runs. Built the one-run-per-project reading, which is what runs live; the slow-stage clause is not built and not tested. Approve to keep that; deny to say this card is right and I will rebuild triage to start beside a running build

behaviour BEH-COST-1 "Kit records what each Claude Code session it runs costs, so weekly spend can be set against the subscription"
  in FEAT-COST
  actor engineer
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-04-1058
  priority 5
  review unreviewed
  pending
  needs-context "Price past token-only sessions at API rates as a marked estimate, or skip them? Until you answer, they are skipped and counted, never priced from their tokens."
  contract my assumption: each session Kit starts keeps its cost, its date, its project and its model, so a week's cost is a sum
  contract my assumption: a new session's cost is the dollar cost its run's own result reports (total_cost_usd), one record per run, written to the database before the runner exits; a failed run's reported cost is kept too
  contract my assumption: past sessions on a dev machine count only where the session record carries a reported dollar cost (costUSD); a session with token usage and no reported cost is skipped and counted, and is not read from ~/.claude/ by the runner or the server
  contract my assumption: weeks run Monday to Sunday, in UTC, and a session counts in the week it started
  contract my assumption: the record is written to the database on Kit's server, outside the project's repo, so no user cost data enters a corpus
  contract my assumption: Kit stores the cost Claude Code reports, not an estimate; the subscription price is not stored by Kit
  contract my assumption: FEAT-COST sits at logic, not ui, while BEH-COST-1 is pending; BEH-STAGE-11 refuses ui for a feature with nothing built, and the built tests are not yet bound to this card

behaviour BEH-TICKET-1 "A ticket is not a spec: tickets appear on the board, and each spec is part of the feature it changes"
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-15-1520
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract my assumption: a ticket represents one change to the spec and carries its status and its open questions; the spec itself lives on the feature branch of that change, not on the board

behaviour BEH-TICKET-2 "A loose item (a note, a chat message, a comment, an approval or a rejection) goes to triage, which turns it into a spec change and a ticket in the new state"
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-15-1520
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract my assumption: triage may create spec, update existing tickets, ask questions, and remove spec that no longer fits the new behaviour
  contract my assumption: triage makes every change on a new feature branch, and a card it removes is marked as to be deleted, with the reason written beside it
  contract my question: approving or rejecting a card does not reach triage today, because an approval only writes the card's review and starts no run. Are these to reach triage as a note? Default until you answer: no; a note or a comment on the card is how he reaches triage

behaviour BEH-TICKET-3 "A ticket with a question for him is blocked, and triage discusses it with him until no question is left, then moves it to ready"
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-15-1520
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract my assumption: blocked is a tag on the ticket, not a separate ticket
  contract my assumption: the discussion stays on that ticket, and the ticket moves to ready once no question is open
  contract my assumption: if he stays silent on a blocked ticket, triage acts on the default written on that ticket
  contract my assumption: silent means he has left no comment on the ticket; the default is the text after "Silence:" in the escalated review's note, which the review stage now writes; triage acts on it at the next triage run of that project (a note of his or a reply on another ticket starts one), and its bundle clears the block
  contract my question: when triage's answer to his reply leaves no question open, nothing of it is posted on the ticket, only his reply is (as BEH-ORCH-16 slice 3 says). Should triage's answer be posted as a comment? Default until you answer: no

behaviour BEH-TICKET-4 "A ready ticket goes next to technical design and then to implementation, and either stage can block the ticket with a question the same way"
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-15-1520
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract my assumption: technical design and implementation use the same blocked tag and ready state as triage
# ── sign-in before use (triage note of 2026-11-07) ──────────────────────────

behaviour BEH-AUTH-1 "A new user must sign in with git before any project page opens"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given user:New
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports access:SignInRequired
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: looking around counts as use, so only the landing page and the sign-in page are open before git sign-in
  contract my assumption: per the owner's reply of 2026-11-19 (ticket sign-in-with-git-before-use), a new user's first sign-in is the username and password he made (BEH-USERS-1), and then the user must connect their GitHub before any project page opens; this card does not replace the password-first onboarding
  contract my assumption: this gate applies only until the user has connected GitHub; after that every sign-in is "Sign in with GitHub" (BEH-USERS-10)

# ── input kept when the pipeline cannot run (triage note of 2026-11-07) ─────

behaviour BEH-KEEP-1 "Chat and note input is kept when credits run out and the pipeline cannot start"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given input:Typed
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports input:Kept
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: "chart" in the note means chat; the typed text stays on the device until the pipeline runs, and is never dropped

# ── tester scope: proposed and contributor projects (triage notes of 2026-11-07) ──

behaviour BEH-SCOPE-1 "A proposed project is kept per user, and a tester sees only their own"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given user:Tester
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports project:OwnProposedOnly
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: a proposed project is stored in the database against its owner until a repo is connected for it, and another user's proposal is never listed
  contract built (tester-feature-access): a tester's list is found through the installation their own GitHub is linked to, so another tester's repos are never in it; the database proposal store is not built, as no proposal flow exists to write to it

behaviour BEH-SCOPE-2 "A project the user is a contributor on is found and listed for that user"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given repo:ContributorListed
  when runs command:KitUi
  then reports project:Listed
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: discovery reads the contributor list of each connected repo, so a project appears only once its repo is connected
  contract my assumption: the project's spec files must sit in that repo, so the tester's holiday planner shows once they are moved there
  contract built (tester-feature-access): a repo is listed for the one user whose installation reaches it, and for no other user, and a repo with no .beh file is not listed; naming a tester as a contributor in the repo is not built, so a repo the tester contributes to through another installation does not appear

# ── the pipeline's pull requests (triage note of 2026-11-07) ────────────────

behaviour BEH-PR-1 "The pipeline raises a pull request for finished work, into the integration branch"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given work:Finished
  when runs command:KitPipeline
  then reports pullRequest:Raised
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: the pull request names the card it builds and targets the project's integration branch, never the default branch

behaviour BEH-PR-2 "The pipeline completes a pull request only when the corpus says it may merge"
  priority 5
  actor engineer
  given pullRequest:Raised
  when runs command:KitPipeline
  then reports pullRequest:Left
  review unreviewed
  contract my assumption: complete means merge, so a pull request is merged only where the corpus allows it, and otherwise is left open for the owner
  contract my question (review request, conflicts with BEH-ORCH-10 in kit-ui.beh): BEH-ORCH-10 merges a built pull request once its Actions checks are green whenever KIT_PIPELINE_MERGE=on, with no corpus line, and this card says it never merges on green checks alone without one. Both cannot hold. Built reading: BEH-ORCH-10 as shipped, unchanged, so nothing merges unless the switch is turned on; no test for this card's merge-without-corpus-line reading is written. Deny it and say which reading is true, and it is rebuilt to that. Default if silent: the built reading stays

behaviour BEH-TICKET-5 "A malformed bundle is named and can be resolved, not left stuck"
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-11-21-1330Z
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract my assumption: a bundle that fails its shape check is shown on the board with the line that is wrong, and no builder starts on it
  contract my assumption: triage rewrites a malformed bundle to the required shape, keeping its cards and notes, and records the fix beside the ticket
